data.content:
Yes — use `Assert.IsInstanceOfType<T>()` which both checks the type and returns the typed instance:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void Create_ReturnsCorrectHandler()
{
    var factory = new HandlerFactory();
    var result = factory.Create("email");

    var handler = Assert.IsInstanceOfType<EmailHandler>(result);
    Assert.AreEqual("email", handler.Type);
}
```

The generic overload (MSTest v3+) returns the cast object, so you can continue asserting on it without a manual cast. If the type doesn't match, you get a clear failure message instead of an `InvalidCastException`.

There's also `Assert.IsNotInstanceOfType<T>()` for the inverse check.
